# Line-ending policy.
#
# Git hooks and shell scripts MUST keep LF endings. Git executes hooks via
# their shebang line, and a CRLF-terminated shebang fails with
# "bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env sh^M". On Windows checkouts with
# core.autocrlf=true this would ship a silently broken hook to every
# consumer, so it is pinned here rather than left to local git config.
#
# Added 2026-07-27 alongside .claude/hooks/commit-msg (PLAN-AD2 W0.7).

# Repo-wide LF, added 2026-07-27.
#
# The two pins below were correct but too narrow. Everything else was
# "unspecified", so local core.autocrlf governed it: git stores LF and
# rewrites the working tree to CRLF on checkout. That left a MIXED working
# tree - files git checked out were CRLF, files the skills' Python helpers
# wrote were LF - and every commit emitted "LF will be replaced by CRLF the
# next time Git touches it" warnings for each one.
#
# Verified before pinning: zero tracked blobs contain CR in the index, so
# this changes nothing that is stored. It only stops the working tree being
# rewritten away from what every tool in this repo writes.
#
# This is a repo of shell hooks, Python helpers and Markdown that agents
# read and rewrite in place. LF everywhere is the ending all of it assumes.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Kept explicit: these are executed rather than merely read, and a
# CRLF-terminated shebang fails with "bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env sh^M".
# Git executes hooks directly, so a broken ending here ships a silently
# dead hook to every consumer. Redundant under the line above by design -
# if that line is ever relaxed, these must not relax with it.

*.sh            text eol=lf
.claude/hooks/** text eol=lf
